course: dictionary
lesson: 1134
title: Describing words that take your breath away
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Breathtaking is an adjective, a describing word. It has two meanings. The first is so beautiful, amazing or exciting that it almost makes you stop breathing. The second is shockingly big or surprising. Context tells you which one is meant.
explain: «Connor hands in an essay about "the breathtaking vistas of the Mediterranean".» Vistas are wide views, and a view that is wide and lovely would make you catch your breath. So here breathtaking means very beautiful. Ask yourself: is the word praising something lovely, or showing that something was shocking? The clues in the sentence will tell you.
explain: Good writers use breathtaking for views, sights, music and sport. But your teacher will warn you not to use it too often. If every view is breathtaking, the word stops meaning much. Try a more exact word, or show the beauty with details: «the sea glittered like broken glass».
fact: Breathtaking is an adjective, so it describes a noun, like a view or a goal.
fact: Its two meanings are: so beautiful, amazing or exciting that it almost stops your breath; and shockingly big or surprising.
fact: «A breathtaking view» and «breathtaking speed» are useful phrases to learn.
fact: «Breathtaking rudeness» shows the second meaning: the rudeness was shockingly big.
fact: Do not overuse breathtaking: save it for the moments that really deserve it.
exercise: «Connor writes about the breathtaking vistas of the Mediterranean.» What does breathtaking mean here? | Hard to read | *So beautiful it almost takes your breath away | Long and boring | Very cold and windy
exercise: «Her rudeness to the referee was breathtaking.» Which meaning fits here? | *Shockingly big or surprising | Slow and steady | Quiet and shy | Lovely and calm
exercise: Find the describing word in «The sunset was breathtaking over the bay.» | sunset | over | bay | *breathtaking
test: Ella writes, «The mountain view was breathtaking.» Why might a teacher say not to use breathtaking too often? | Because it is a rude word | *Because it loses its power when used everywhere | Because it only means cold | Because it is too short to count
test: Which sentence uses breathtaking to mean shockingly big or surprising? | The music was breathtaking and made me cry | *The price rise was breathtaking, far more than anyone expected | The lake was breathtaking in the evening sun | The cake was breathtaking, a soft sponge with cream
exam: Jayden writes «a breathtaking view» and «breathtaking speed». Which statement is correct? | The first means rude, and the second means quiet | Both mean the same as cold and wet | Breathtaking can only ever be used for music | *The first is about beauty, and the second is about something surprisingly fast
